<p>
We are located right next to the famous <span style="font-weight:bold;">Miracle on 34th Street</span> in the Hampden neighborhood of Baltimore.
</p>

<p>
It is one of Baltimore’s best-known holiday traditions:

an entire residential block covered in elaborate Christmas lights and decorations
located on the 700 block of West 34th Street
between Keswick Road and Chestnut Avenue.
</p>

<p>
The street is lined with classic Baltimore rowhouses, and during the holidays nearly every house becomes part of a huge community art display:
thousands of lights,
animated decorations,
themed displays,
Baltimore cultural references,
whimsical homemade art.
</p>

<p>
Some famous features include:
the “hubcap Christmas tree”,
bicycle-wheel snowmen,
giant crabs,
Natty Boh imagery,
trains and moving displays.
</p>

<p>
It feels
quirky,
festive,
very Baltimore.
Not polished like a commercial light show — more handmade, neighborhood-driven, and creative.
The experience is usually
crowded,
energetic,
family-oriented,
nostalgic.
</p>

<p>
Walk the block slowly,
take photos,
drink hot chocolate,
visit nearby Hampden restaurants and shops.
The neighborhood itself has a distinctive personality:
artsy,
eclectic,
old Baltimore rowhouse character.
</p>

<p>
Lights begin just after Thanksgiving,
continue through New Year’s Day,
turn on around dusk nightly.
Better to walk than drive.
</p>

<p>
Most locals recommend:
parking nearby
then walking.
Many people combine the lights with:
dinner,
dessert,
shopping on “The Avenue” (36th Street)--
The whole area becomes a holiday outing.
</p>


<p>
Imagine
narrow rowhouses
lights strung roof-to-roof across the street,
colorful decorations everywhere,
glowing displays packed tightly together.
</p>

<p>
It has become
a Baltimore tradition for decades.
It is a nationally known,
cultural icon in the city.
Many Baltimore residents consider
“The Miracle on 34th Street”
the unofficial start of Christmas season.


